  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>Can Ravens Talk?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens can mimic human speech and produce recognisable words, but they are not "talking" in the linguistic sense. Captive ravens, especially those raised from chicks, learn to produce human-sounding words and phrases through vocal mimicry. Wild ravens do not normally produce human words but can mimic a wide range of environmental sounds — other animals, tools, vehicles, and human voices.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Vocalisation — What They Can Do</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens have one of the most complex vocal repertoires of any non-parrot bird. Their calls include deep croaks, hollow "kronk" calls, a gurgling water-drip sound, wooden knocking, and a wide variety of contact and alarm calls.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are vocal learners: they learn their calls from other ravens and from their environment, rather than having a fully innate repertoire. This capacity for vocal learning is the same mechanism that enables human speech mimicry.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Mimicry in Captive Ravens</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Captive ravens raised in close contact with humans can learn to produce human-sounding words. This is documented in aviculture, keeper reports, and several scientific accounts. The most cited historical example is Dickens' pet raven "Grip", which reportedly said "Halloa, old girl!" and other phrases — though these are keeper reports, not controlled experiments.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>Well-documented captive examples show ravens producing words that are recognisable to human listeners, though often with raven-characteristic tonal qualities. The mimicry is real; the question of whether it carries meaning is more complex.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Wild Raven Mimicry</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In the wild, ravens mimic other species — the calls of other corvids, raptors, and mammals — apparently to manipulate them. For example, ravens in some areas have been observed mimicking wolf howls, possibly to attract wolves to carcasses the ravens cannot open alone. They also mimic sounds associated with food sources.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens vs Parrots for Speech</h2>
    <p>Ravens are impressive mimics, but parrots — particularly African Greys (<em>Psittacus erithacus</em>) — are superior in terms of clarity, volume range, and depth of vocal repertoire for human speech. The comparison is sometimes made in popular writing; in controlled tests, parrot speech mimicry is more accurate and consistent than raven mimicry.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-smart-are-ravens/">How smart are ravens?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — species profile</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
